**Photos**
Create 5,000 distinct, descriptive photo titles. Captions must accurately describe the associated image subject and must not be random combinations of unrelated words.

**Users**
Create 10 coherent fictional user profiles with natural full names, professional usernames, matching example-domain email addresses, realistic international phone-number formatting, city/address information, websites, and company descriptions.

Use fictional profiles and clearly avoid presenting fabricated contact details as verified real people or businesses.

## 4. Real, Working HD Photography URLs — Critical Requirement

The photo URLs are the most important part of this task.

Every photo must use a **real, existing photograph hosted on the Unsplash image CDN**. Do not use placeholder services, fake URLs, invented image hashes, local color blocks, SVG placeholders, or URLs that merely look valid.

Use this URL pattern with verified Unsplash photo IDs:

Full image:
`https://images.unsplash.com/photo-VERIFIED_PHOTO_ID?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=600&q=80`

Thumbnail:
`https://images.unsplash.com/photo-VERIFIED_PHOTO_ID?auto=format&fit=crop&w=150&h=150&q=80`

Replace `VERIFIED_PHOTO_ID` with an actual verified photo identifier. These URLs are examples of the required format, not complete URLs.

Requirements:
- Both URLs must point to the same underlying photograph.
- Only the dimensions and optimization parameters should differ between the full image and thumbnail.
- Images must load successfully and return a valid image response.
- Use genuine photography covering people, portraits, nature, landscapes, architecture, cities, technology, offices, food, and travel.
- Match each photo's title to the actual subject of its image.
- Reuse verified image IDs only when necessary; prioritize variety.
- Do not invent 5,000 unique photo IDs or assume that changing URL parameters creates a different photograph.
- Do not generate random Unsplash URLs without validating that the image exists.

If 5,000 verified, distinct photographs are not realistically obtainable, use a curated collection of verified image IDs and clearly report the number of unique images. Never fake verification or claim all 5,000 images are unique when they are not.

Add a validation script that checks image HTTP responses, image content types, and URL consistency. Avoid making thousands of unnecessary requests on every application startup; provide a separate validation command.

